WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.640
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:11.600
Today we are going to design a crazy baseball&nbsp;
bat, a hockey stick that’s not working well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:18.560
springs that won’t stop bouncing, and&nbsp;
letters that can kind of stop bouncing.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:26.400
And, yes, you see it correctly, this&nbsp;
is a paper from 2017. Why? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:32.880
there are some good works that are timeless.&nbsp;
This is one of them. You’ll see why in a moment.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:38.240
When I read this paper, I saw that it is&nbsp;
from professor Doug James’s group at Stanford&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:45.040
University, and at this point, I knew that crazy&nbsp;
things are to be expected. Our seasoned Fellow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:52.080
Scholars know that these folks do some absolutely&nbsp;
amazing things that shouldn’t even be possible.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:59.040
For instance, one of their earlier papers takes an&nbsp;
animation and the physics data for these bubbles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:06.160
and, impossible as it might appear,&nbsp;
synthesizes the sound of these bubbles. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:11.840
video goes in, sound comes out.&nbsp;

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:22.640
And hold on to your papers because that’s&nbsp;
nothing compared to their other work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:29.680
which takes not the video, but the sound&nbsp;
that we recorded. So, the sound goes in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:39.840
that’s easy enough, and what does it do? Creates&nbsp;
a video animation that matches these sounds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:50.240
That’s a crazy paper that works exceptionally&nbsp;
well. We love those around here.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:56.080
And, to top it off, both of these were handcrafted&nbsp;
techniques, no machine learning is applied.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:03.440
So, what about today’s paper? Today’s paper&nbsp;
is about designing things that don’t work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:09.040
or things that work too well. What&nbsp;
does that mean? Well, let me explain.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:14.880
Here is a map that shows the physical&nbsp;
bounciness parameter for a not too exciting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:21.840
old simulation. Everything, and every part&nbsp;
does the same. And here is the new method.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:25.040
Red means bouncy, blue means stiff.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:32.640
And now, on to the simulation. The old one&nbsp;
with the fixed parameters. Not bad, but it not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:41.360
too exciting either. And, here is the new one.&nbsp;
Now that is a simulation has some personality.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:48.480
So, with this, we can reimagine as if parts&nbsp;
of things were made of rubber, and other parts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:56.320
of wood or steel. Look. Here, the red color on the&nbsp;
knob of the baseball bat means that this will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:03.680
made bouncy, while the end will be made stiff.
What does this do? Well, let’s see together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:09.920
This is the reference simulation where&nbsp;
every part has the same bounciness. And now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:15.840
let’s see that bouncy knob. There we go!&nbsp;
With this, we can unleash our artistic vision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:21.600
and get a simulation that works properly,&nbsp;
given these crazy material parameters.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:27.520
Now, let’s design a crazy hockey stick. This&nbsp;
part of the hockey stick is very bouncy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:33.920
this part too, however, this part will be&nbsp;
the sweet spot, at least for this experiment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:36.640
Let’s hit that puck and see how they behave.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:43.760
Yes, from the red, bouncy regions, indeed,&nbsp;
the puck rebounds a great deal. And now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:50.480
let’s see the sweet spot. Yes, it rebounds&nbsp;
much less. Let’s see all of them side by side.

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:55.200
Now, creating such an algorithm&nbsp;
is quite a challenge. Look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:01.280
it has to work on smooth geometry, built&nbsp;
from hundreds of thousands of triangles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:07.760
And one of the key challenges is that the&nbsp;
duration of the contacts can be… what?! Are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:15.200
you seeing what I am seeing? The duration of some&nbsp;
of these contacts is measured in the order of tens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:23.200
of microseconds. And, it still works well, and&nbsp;
it’s still accurate. That is absolutely amazing.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:28.960
Now, of course, even though we can have&nbsp;
detailed geometry made of crazy new materials,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:34.800
this is not only a great tool for artists.&nbsp;
This could also help with contact analysis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:41.680
and other cool engineering applications where we&nbsp;
manufacture things that hit each other. Glorious.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:48.560
So this is an amazing, timeless work from 2017,&nbsp;
and I am worried that if we don’t talk about it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:54.880
here on Two Minute Papers, almost no one will&nbsp;
talk about it. And these works are so good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.880 --> 00:05:00.320
people have to know! Thank you very much for&nbsp;
watching this, and let’s spread the word together!

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:54.480
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

